  :adr/body "## The report

ログインできました。slash model コマンドを押しても model 一覧が出てこない、選択
できない。

## The command was answering a different question

`/model` fetched `/api/bots/model-routing` and printed it as EDN. That document
is a list of TASKS and their assignments:

    {:default-scope \"default\" :main-task \"bot\"
     :tasks [{:task \"bot\" :label \"Bot\" …} {:task \"chat\" …} …]
     :assignments []}

It does not contain a single model name. The models are somewhere else
entirely -- in the Bots overview, under `:model-providers`, one entry per
provider with a `:models` vector. So the screen printed a document nobody
asked for, showed no models, and offered nothing to pick. With `assignments`
empty it was almost entirely punctuation.

The 403 fixed yesterday was real and was not the whole fault: with a human
session the same command would still have shown no models.

## What it is now

A panel, tabs per task (`Bot ★` / Bot同士の会話 / この機械の操作 / チャット),
items being every provider-and-model pair this deployment can reach, the one
in force on that task marked `●`, ↑↓ to move and enter to set it.

`POST /api/bots/model-routing` takes `{:task :scope :provider-id :model}` and
neither Origin nor CSRF is required for a bearer request -- both exist because
a browser attaches a cookie by itself, and nothing attaches a bearer token by
itself.

## Panels can now be chosen from

`:items` on a tab makes it a list rather than text: ↑↓ move a selection instead
of a scrollbar, the selection is kept on screen when the list is longer than
the frame, and switching tabs starts the selection again -- carrying index 2
into a tab with one item would point at nothing.

Enter on a list produces a VALUE -- `:chose {:panel … :item …}` -- which the
REPL acts on. The editor stays pure: a panel that acted for itself would put an
HTTP call inside `handle`, and every key semantic in this file would stop being
testable. Enter on a text panel still just closes.

## One more thing that was dropping answers

`on-submit!` unwrapped a promise's result only when it was a keyword. `/model`
fetches before it can build its panel, so it answers late with a map -- and the
map went on the floor. The command ran, fetched twice, built the panel, and the
screen showed nothing. A handler may now answer late with anything it could
have answered with at once.

## Measured

On a pty against the live resident: `/model` opens with `[Bot ★]` selected and
six models listed; ↓ moves the marker; enter sets it and the server then
reports

    assignments: [{\"task\":\"bot\",\"scope\":\"default\",
                   \"provider-id\":\"murakumo\",\"model\":\"murakumo-main\"}]

where it had been empty. Two controls, each failing by name: enter on a list
that only closes, and a tab change that keeps the old index.

## Not claimed

The list is what the server offers. Two of the six -- `z-ai/glm-5.3-flash` and
`qwen/qwen3.8-flash` -- answered `external_model_requires_payment` when
measured this morning, and choosing one will produce the failure recorded in
adr-2609071800. The panel does not mark them, because that measurement was
this morning's and a screen that prints yesterday's reachability as though it
were today's is the thing this repository keeps writing rules about.
"}]
